Oh, I see. So [here is](https://github.com/mavlink/MAVSDK/blob/main/src/mavsdk/plugins/offboard/offboard_impl.cpp#L46) what `offboard->start();` does:

```c++
Offboard::Result OffboardImpl::start()
{
    {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(_mutex);
        if (_mode == Mode::NotActive) {
            return Offboard::Result::NoSetpointSet;
        }
        _last_started = _time.steady_time();
    }

    return offboard_result_from_command_result(
        _parent->set_flight_mode(SystemImpl::FlightMode::Offboard));
}
```

I see 3 things being done there:

1. Check that the plugin is "active", in the sense that some setpoint has been set. You "activate" it when you call [`set_velocity_body`](https://github.com/mavlink/MAVSDK/blob/main/src/mavsdk/plugins/offboard/offboard_impl.cpp#L283).
2. Set the `_last_started` value.
3. Set the flight mode to `Offboard`, which is what you do with your RC switch.

So let's look at what this `_last_started` [does](https://github.com/mavlink/MAVSDK/blob/main/src/mavsdk/plugins/offboard/offboard_impl.cpp#L784):

```c++
        // Right after we started offboard we might still be getting heartbeats
        // from earlier which don't indicate offboard mode yet.
        // Therefore, we make sure we don't stop too eagerly and ignore
        // possibly stale heartbeats for some time.
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(_mutex);
        if (!offboard_mode_active && _mode != Mode::NotActive &&
            _time.elapsed_since_s(_last_started) > 3.0) {
            // It seems that we are no longer in offboard mode but still trying to send
            // setpoints. Let's stop for now.
            stop_sending_setpoints();
        }
```

My understanding here is that if we are not in `Offboard` mode anymore, then MAVSDK calls `stop_sending_setpoints();` (after 3 seconds since the last `start` call) :thinking:. And `stop_sending_setpoints` moves it back to "not active".

So... short answer is that what you want to do is not supported: MAVSDK currently _wants_ `offboard->start()` to be called. More importantly, MAVSDK needs a way to detect when it should stop sending the setpoints.

I am not completely sure how to solve that: how can MAVSDK know that you plan on starting the offboard mode later from a different channel? Or should MAVSDK always send the setpoints when the autopilot is in `Offboard` mode unless `offboard->stop()` has been called explicitly? Maybe that could make sense indeed :thinking:.

@julianoes would you have an opinion on that? I guess mostly in terms of safety (we don't want MAVSDK to do weird stuff with offboard mode and cause a crash, obviously).

